# Content conventions

- `content/source/cv.yaml` is read-only fact. Never edit, normalize,
  correct, or write to it.
- Every project/decision manifest validates against a versioned public JSON
  Schema. Unknown or missing required fields fail the build, not just a
  lint warning.
- Bilingual content (English/Spanish) is fact-checked, contextual
  translation, not a mechanical mirror. Rephrasing for a natural register is
  allowed; every factual claim in either language must still trace to
  `cv.yaml` or an approved `content/public-sources/` entry.
- Every non-obvious factual claim carries a citation: a `cv.yaml` source
  path, a `content/public-sources/` entry, or a `cv:sync`-generated
  provenance record. A claim without a traceable source does not ship.
- Project/decision status (`planned`, `building`, `verified`, ...) comes
  only from a validated manifest field, never freehand copy. `verified` is
  never set without recorded evidence behind it.
- Any personal-case asset (photo, screenshot, credential image) is an
  approved, existing asset. No AI-generated photograph of Diego, ever; use a
  labelled placeholder until a real one is supplied.
- `pnpm cv:sync` is a manual, read-only operation against the separate CV
  repository. It copies the PDF and produces a preview, public JSON, and a
  manifest recording the source commit and SHA-256 digest. It never edits
  `content/source/cv.yaml`.
- All scenario, fixture, and Ask Diego corpus content is visibly synthetic or
  drawn only from approved sources — never real visitor input.
